WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.775
Task statement 2.3.

2
00:00:01.775 --> 00:00:06.540
Distribute tools appropriately across agents and configure tool choice.

3
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:16.137
A research synthesis agent that also holds web search tools will start searching instead of writing, and selection gets worse as tools pile up.

4
00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:20.320
Tool selection gets harder as the list grows.

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.576
An agent with eighteen tools picks worse than one with four or five well chosen ones.

6
00:00:25.576 --> 00:00:30.771
And an agent that holds tools outside its job will use them, because they are there.

7
00:00:30.771 --> 00:00:36.460
So treat each agent like a kitchen station, stocked only for the dish it is responsible for.

8
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.095
Scope tools by role.

9
00:00:39.095 --> 00:00:47.429
In the Agent SDK each subagent definition carries its own tools list, and anything you leave out simply is not available to that agent.

10
00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:57.060
When a role frequently needs a little of another role's power, give it a small purpose-built tool, like a claim checker, instead of the full search toolkit.

11
00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.811
Tool choice controls whether a tool call is optional.

12
00:01:01.811 --> 00:01:05.161
Auto lets Claude decide and may answer in plain text.

13
00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:07.374
Any guarantees some tool is called.

14
00:01:07.374 --> 00:01:09.713
Naming a tool forces that exact tool.

15
00:01:09.713 --> 00:01:14.138
A common pattern is to force the first step, then switch back to auto.

16
00:01:14.138 --> 00:01:19.196
The newest models reject any and forced, but the exam keeps the guide's options.

17
00:01:20.596 --> 00:01:24.455
In the research system, the synthesizer drifts into searching.

18
00:01:24.455 --> 00:01:29.869
Take the search tools away and give it one narrow verify tool for the occasional check.

19
00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:38.272
In an extraction step that must always produce structured data, use any, or force the extraction tool, so Claude cannot reply in prose.

20
00:01:38.272 --> 00:01:41.695
Then relax to auto once the required step has happened.

21
00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:45.309
The exam rewards structural fixes.

22
00:01:45.309 --> 00:01:50.975
If an agent misuses a tool, take the tool away rather than asking nicely in the prompt.

23
00:01:50.975 --> 00:01:54.231
If a call is mandatory, auto is the wrong setting.

24
00:01:54.231 --> 00:02:00.417
And forcing should usually be temporary, just long enough to guarantee the critical first step.

25
00:02:01.817 --> 00:02:02.395
To recap.

26
00:02:02.395 --> 00:02:08.882
Give each agent a small, role-shaped set of tools, with narrow helpers for frequent cross-role needs.

27
00:02:08.882 --> 00:02:11.259
Remove tools an agent should not use.

28
00:02:11.259 --> 00:02:19.993
Know the three tool choice settings cold, and remember the pattern of forcing only the first required step before relaxing back to auto.

